  "keyInsight": "A vertical asymptote describes what happens near a forbidden x-value, while a slant asymptote describes what the rational function looks like as x goes far left or far right.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Use a single 2D Cartesian graph with a white background and black axes crossing at the origin. Plot the rational curve in dark blue, preferably matching f(x) = 3x + 1 + 2/(x - 1), with a gap at x = 1. Draw the vertical asymptote as a dashed orange line at x = 1 and label it in orange near the bottom. Draw the slant asymptote as a dashed teal line y = 3x + 1 and label it in teal near the middle-right of the graph. The left curve branch should hug the slant line at the lower left, crest slightly below the x-axis, and plunge downward near x = 1. The right branch should enter from above just right of x = 1, dip to a minimum around y = 9, and rise toward the upper right while tracking the slant line. In curve-only mode hide both dashed asymptotes and labels; in asymptotes-only mode hide the blue curve; in combined mode show all. The probe should avoid plotting exactly at x = 1 and may display an undefined warning or snap to the nearest valid side if the slider lands on the asymptote."
